    "lawCode": "Rpld §217 sub (f), Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5273\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to repeal subdivision (f) of section 217 of the\ngeneral municipal law relating to service award programs\n\nPURPOSE:  To eliminate a service award penalty imposed upon certain\npublic employees who perform duties as volunteer firefighters.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Repeal subdivision 217 (f) of the general municipal law.\n\nSection 2. Overrides any provisions in local service award program plans\nwhich implemented and enforced subdivision 217 (f) repealed by this act.\n\nSection 3. Effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  In 1988, article 11-a was added to the general municipal\nlaw (gml) to authorize municipalities and fire districts to establish\nLength of Service Award Programs (LOSAPs). Presently, approximately 600\nsuch plans are operated in New York to incent recruitment and retention\nof volunteer firefighters. Although the service awards provided pursuant\nto LOSAPs are related to credits earned annually by performing various\nvolunteer firefighter functions for a period of years and are paid on a\nmonthly basis upon the participating volunteer reaching a certain age,\nLOSAPs are not \"pension plans\" and the receipt of such awards are not\n\"pension benefits\" for any federal or state purposes.\n\nThe extensive commitment to preparation for and active participation in\nproviding emergency services to one's community is not, in any real\nsense, recompensed by the availability of small LOSAP payments to long-\nserving, dedicated volunteers but it may be perceived as minimal incen-\ntive to commence and to continue undertaking such training and providing\nessential public service.\n\nSubdivision 217 (f) of the gml, ineffectually and unfairly penalizes all\npublic employees who serve their state or community as paid civil serv-\nants and separately as volunteer firefighters. Section 217 (f) of the\ngml precludes a volunteer firefighter who provides firefighter services\n(generally emergency services) during his or her \"regularly assigned\nwork periods\" from receiving LOSAP credit for those services. So, in\nspite of the increasingly desperate need to attract folks willing to\nundergo hundreds of hours of required training and stay active for many,\nmany years to earn any marginally-significant LOSAP benefits, public\nemployees are \"disincented\" from providing volunteer firefighter\nservices for circumstances over which they have no control and which may\nalready cause them significant employment-related penalties. Individual\npublic employees have no control over whether their employer will allow\nthem to respond to a structural fire in their community during their\n'regularly assigned work period' what leave credits will be charged for\ntheir absence from work, whether additional (\"make-up\") work will be\nrequired of them or any other issues under the exclusive control of\n\ntheir public employer. In order for a public employee to earn State or\nNYC pension credit for hours worked or leave credits used, those hours\nmust be reported to the respective pension system; \"regularly assigned\nwork periods\" have no meaning or relevance to pension credit accrual or\nemployer reporting.\n\nIndividual volunteer firefighters have no control over when their commu-\nnity will need to access their trained and sometimes life risking\nskills. The administration of a LOSAP plan cannot, rationally be contin-\ngent upon fire officers questioning each volunteer in the aftermath of a\nfire emergency as to whether they had reported for duty during a 'regu-\nlarly assigned work period\" as a public employee and then reporting this\nto a plan administrator. Therefore, it is highly likely that this exist-\ning law \"is honored more in its breach than its observance\". Two volun-\nteer firefighters responding side-by-side to an emergency in their\ncommunity should earn the same LOSAP credits for doing so without a\npenalty being imposed upon one who is a public servant who may or may\nnot be a participant in a pension system and who will be subject to\nwhatever constraints, sanctions or requirements a public employer may\nimpose in relation to their time away from work.\n\nDiligent research has failed to disclose similar public employee penal-\nties in other states which have LOSAP programs except to note that New\nJersey initially had a virtually identical provision in their LOSAP law\nbut promptly repealed it after a couple of years because it was ineffec-\ntual and ufair, New Jersey Public Law 2001 Chapter 272. Subdivision 217\n(f) is unfair, ineffectual and antithetical to the public policy\nembodied in the remainder of article 11-a of the gml; it should be\nrepealed.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
